 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per Gaussian-pixel pair. The operations involve matrix-vector multiplications and a single exponential evaluation [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory per evaluation [G].
-- **Performance:** The method is computationally lightweight per pixel but is called for every Gaussian-pixel intersection. In a full rasterization pass, the cost is proportional to the number of splats per pixel [G].
-- **Strengths:** Simple, differentiable, and efficient for sparse scenes [G].
-- **Limitations:** Does not handle occlusion or compositing order; these are handled in subsequent steps. The approximation of the projection using the Jacobian can introduce artifacts for large Gaussians or extreme viewing angles [G].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per Gaussian-pixel pair.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory.
+- **Strengths:** Simple, differentiable, and efficient for single-point evaluation.
+- **Limitations:** Does not handle batch operations or culling; requires explicit pixel-Gaussian intersection checks in a full rasterizer.
 
 ## Background
-The method is a core component of 3D Gaussian Splatting, a technique for real-time radiance field rendering [G]. It was introduced in the context of learning 3D Gaussian primitives from images [G]. The specific formulation of projecting 3D Gaussians to 2D and computing alpha values is detailed in the implementation of Gaussian Splatting [S1, unofficial]. The underlying mathematical model of using a Gaussian kernel as a Point Spread Function (PSF) is also discussed in the context of differentiable rendering for fluorescence microscopy [S2, unofficial].
+Originated in the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting for differentiable rendering [S1, unofficial]. It is a fundamental component of the rasterization pipeline, enabling the computation of alpha values for compositing.
 
